assets.prompt:
Create a finished Tolkienesque fantasy-atlas map of a real Great Lakes region reimagined for 2075. Use case: stylized-concept. Asset: a beautiful explorable regional world map, landscape 3:2, detailed ink and restrained wash on warm ivory drawing paper. Title exactly "THE GLASS BASIN" and small subtitle "Western Basin · 2075". Image 1 is GEOGRAPHIC REFERENCE ONLY: preserve the north-up relative shoreline, lake, Toledo river-mouth context, mainland Port Clinton coast and island relationship. Ignore its watershed polygon edges/colors. Image 2 is ART STYLE ONLY: use its fine architectural ink, faded blue wash, natural paper texture and detailed little bridge/roof/boat/reed drawings; DO NOT copy its erroneous map, six regions, text or technologies.
The map should feel like an inviting fantasy novel endpaper by an excellent cartographer, with small bird's-eye town silhouettes, glass-canopy roofs, old bridge forms, industrial crane yards, low fields with drainage channels, woodlots and restrained marsh reeds. Flat regional land, no mountains or fantasy castles. Lake Erie across the upper right, the Maumee curving from lower left toward Toledo, mainland working coast extending east/southeast toward Port Clinton. Catawba stays a mainland peninsula. Do not detach it as an island. Only loose fading character areas, no colored territorial polygons, no border walls and no giant blanket swamp.
Place FIVE unmistakable hand-lettered regional names across spacious appropriate areas, exactly:
"GLASS CITY CORE" around Toledo near the river mouth;
"MAUMEE RIVER COMMONS" following the river southwest;
"LAKE ERIE ENERGY & SECURITY COAST" along the mainland coast east of Toledo;
"BLACK SWAMP COUNTRY" over the open agricultural lower half, scattered reeds not universal swamp;
"GREAT LAKES INDUSTRIAL BELT" along branching yards/corridors east and south of Toledo.
Keep these names readable, without overlapping buildings. Small real reference labels only "Toledo", "Waterville", "Port Clinton", "Lake Erie", "Maumee River". Symbolic future architecture is a vignette, not a exact-site map pin. A small compass rose, cartouche and restrained corner ink ornament. Delicate lake wave marks, occasional faded ochre field wash, muted blue and warm black ink. Atmospheric, crafted, precise enough to recognize this place, not GIS styling or a dashboard. No people, no stick figures, no dragons, no neon, no literal futuristic tower forest, no engineering dimensions, no legend clutter or numeric scale bar. The map is imagined regional character, not a scientific measurement.
